Espressif’s QEMU fork supports running/debugging apps without flashing real hardware, and the ESP32-S3 QEMU docs mention a virtual RGB panel/framebuffer path. That is very interesting for GUI work. ([Espressif Systems][1]) ([GitHub][2])

## Best plan

Build the app around **three backends**:

### 1. ESP32-QEMU backend

Purpose: real firmware execution.

Input:

```text
firmware.bin
bootloader.bin
partition-table.bin
```

Run through Espressif QEMU.

Good for:

```text
Serial logs
boot behaviour
FreeRTOS behaviour
timing-ish bugs
crashes/panics
basic peripheral testing
GDB debugging
```

### 2. Arduino display shim backend

Purpose: fast TFT UI development.

Compile the sketch for native Linux using mocked Arduino APIs:

```cpp
Serial
SPI
Wire
digitalWrite
pinMode
millis
delay
TFT_eSPI
LovyanGFX
Adafruit_GFX
PNGdec
TJpg_Decoder
```

Then render to SDL/WebView.

Good for:

```text
ILI9341
ST7789
ST7796
GC9A01
SSD1306
touch simulation
screenshots
frame recording
layout debugging
```

This is probably the most valuable part for you.

### 3. Hybrid mode

Firmware runs in QEMU, but display writes are exported to your app.

Possible routes:

```text
QEMU UART bridge -> display command protocol
QEMU virtual framebuffer -> app window
instrumented Arduino display library -> socket/WebSocket
QEMU peripheral hook -> SPI decoder
```

The most practical first version is:

```text
Arduino sketch uses TFT_eSPI-compatible simulator driver
driver sends draw commands over localhost
desktop app renders the display
```

## Recommended MVP

Do **not** start with USB impersonation. Start with:

```text
Board profile: ESP32 Dev Module
Framework: Arduino
Display: ILI9341 / ST7789
Build tool: PlatformIO or arduino-cli
Run modes:
  - Native display simulator
  - QEMU serial simulator
```

Then later add fake upload support.

The app flow:

```text
Open project folder
Select board: esp32dev
Select display: ILI9341 320x240
Click Build & Run
App calls pio run / arduino-cli compile
App launches simulator
Virtual TFT appears
Serial monitor appears
SPI/GPIO logs appear
```

